Z||000000||86SEFIERO||06-15-2000||03:33 AM||||<BR>Whose got v-8 fieros? how much horsepower?Manual or automatic? How much cash can one expext to spend if install themselves?||||reg||1
Z||000001||batboy||06-15-2000||07:31 AM||||I bought my V8 Fiero as a half finished project, so I can't tell you how hard it was to install. It has an automatic tranny. I will say that it's been very time consuming working out all the bugs. I also spent lots of time and money on the suspension, you have to in order for it to handle good with all that extra weight of a small block in the back. My current engine is a half wore out stock 350 truck motor with an Edelbrock Preformer carb. I'm guessing not much more than 200-220 HP and maybe 280-300 TQ. I have a much better engine planned for the future, a 1988 Z-28 roller 4-bolt main 350 with TRW forged flat-top pistons and Comp Cams roller valve train. What's holding me up right now is the heads. I need to buy a good set of aluminum high performance heads, but they are very expensive. Eventually I get enough saved up to buy them and then look out!||||reg||1
Z||000002||Eric||06-15-2000||11:51 AM||||What do you think the weight gain is from a V6 to a V8 smallblock with aluminum heads and carb?  I'm guessing the heads weigh about the same and the blocks are about 60lbs different.  You wouldn't have all that heavy fuel injection hardware like the big manifolds and such.  A little more oil in the V8.  I would say the V8 wouldn't even tip the scales at 80lbs more than the V6.  Move the battery up front and you have roughly the same weight distribution as the V6.  Then add some slightly stiffer springs and bigger tires.  I think you could come out ahead of the game suspension wise even with a bulky V8.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Spyder||06-15-2000||10:56 PM||||I installed a V8, Horsepower is somewhere between 275 and 300 with a 5 speed manual. The price, I have no idea. I would save up, buy parts, save up, buy more parts.  I’ve done a lot of extra stuff.||||reg||1
